From: cjom <cjom@reglinux.org>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/probe-helper: cap the advertised mode list at 1080p60

Filter in drm_helper_probe_single_connector_modes(), the one point every
mode passes through whatever its origin. The old drm_edid.c pixel-clock
filter missed CEA VIC modes entirely, so a 4K TV still offered 2160p60.

REG-Linux has no use for modes above 1080p or above 60 Hz, so both are
capped here. Rejections keep distinct statuses -- MODE_VIRTUAL_Y for
resolution, MODE_VSYNC for refresh -- so the existing mode-rejection
debug output stays readable.

Internal panels (DSI/eDP/DPI/LVDS) must stay exempt: their modes come from
the panel driver, so filtering leaves an empty mode list and no display at
all -- an earlier revision bricked every sm8250 handheld. Both axes must
exceed the resolution cap so it is orientation-independent: 1080x1920
portrait and 2560x1080 ultrawide pass, 2560x1440 and 3840x2160 do not.

Signed-off-by: Romain Tisserand <romain.tisserand@gmail.com>
[cjom: replace the EDID pixel-clock filter with a probe-helper mode cap]
Signed-off-by: cjom <cjom@reglinux.org>

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_probe_helper.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_probe_helper.c
@@ -470,6 +470,24 @@
 		if (mode->status != MODE_OK)
 			continue;
 
+		/* REG-Linux: cap the mode list at 1080p60. Internal panels are
+		 * exempt -- filtering their driver-supplied list would leave no
+		 * modes at all. Both axes must exceed the resolution cap, so
+		 * portrait 1080x1920 and 2560x1080 ultrawide still pass. */
+		if (connector->connector_type != DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_DSI &&
+		    connector->connector_type != DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_eDP &&
+		    connector->connector_type != DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_DPI &&
+		    connector->connector_type != DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_LVDS) {
+			if (mode->hdisplay > 1080 && mode->vdisplay > 1080) {
+				mode->status = MODE_VIRTUAL_Y;
+				continue;
+			}
+			if (drm_mode_vrefresh(mode) > 60) {
+				mode->status = MODE_VSYNC;
+				continue;
+			}
+		}
+
 		mode->status = drm_mode_validate_flag(mode, mode_flags);
 		if (mode->status != MODE_OK)
 			continue;
